fresh clone, so that the text carried here is byte-for-byte the text the AOT
build compiles. Seventeen copies of that tree exist under `target/`, one per
build hash; all seventeen were compared and are identical.

### What is carried

Twenty-eight files: the transitive `#include` closure, inside the FlashInfer
tree, of the fifteen headers `crates/kernels-cuda/csrc/src` names --
`attention/{cascade,decode,mask,mla,prefill,scheduler,state,variants,
default_decode_params,default_prefill_params}.cuh` and
`{fastdiv,layout,page,pos_enc,utils}.cuh`. 18,207 lines.

Not carried, and deliberately: `attention/hopper/*`, `comm/*`,
`gemm/bmm_fp8.cuh`, `cutlass_utils.cuh` and `mamba/selective_state_update.cuh`.
They are outside the closure of those fifteen seeds, they are a different
architecture's problem (sm_90 TMA, NVSHMEM, CUTLASS), and carrying text
nobody compiles would be weight in every binary.

### What was changed

Nothing but guards. **35 `#ifndef __CUDACC_RTC__` guards across 14 of the 28
files, 226 added lines**; the other 14 files are byte-identical to upstream.
Every copyright header is intact and untouched. Nothing was reformatted,
renamed, reordered or "improved" -- include order in particular is upstream's,
which is why several files carry four small guards where one large one would
have needed a line moved.

A guard exists because NVRTC compiles for the device only. It refuses a
function marked `__host__` outright ("a function explicitly marked as a
__host__ function is not allowed in JIT mode"), it has no C++ standard
library, and it has no include path -- so `<iostream>`, `<sstream>`,
`<vector>`, the `cudaLaunchKernel` wrappers and the `std::ostringstream` error
paths have to be hidden from it. They are hidden, not deleted: the ahead-of-
time build defines no `__CUDACC_RTC__` and therefore sees upstream exactly.

Each guard is preceded by a marker of a fixed form:

    // PIE: guarded for NVRTC -- <what and why>. Upstream is unmodified apart
    // from markers of this form; see NOTICE. Removing them restores the file.

One `grep -rn "// PIE:" csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/` finds all 35. The
boilerplate sentence appears once per file, on its first marker.
`csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/MODIFICATIONS` lists the count per file, and the
property that list asserts -- *strip the markers, the `#ifndef` under each and
its matching `#endif`, and every file is byte-for-byte upstream* -- was checked
mechanically, per file, against the CPM tree before this NOTICE was written.
`tests/upstream_manifest.rs` re-checks the half of that claim which needs no
network: every file's guard count, added-line count, line count and byte count
still match the table, and the strip is still well defined for every file. The
other half -- that the stripped text equals upstream's -- needs the CPM
checkout and is a manual step on rebase.

Two consequences are worth recording because they are not visible in a diff:

  * `attention/scheduler.cuh` is guarded in its entirety, all 1,808 lines of
    it. It is the host planner -- `std::vector` bin-packing, workspace
    carving, `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor` -- and the three
    `__global__` declarations at its top are forward declarations that exist
    only to give the occupancy query a function pointer. There is no device
    code in it to keep. Under the JIT the plan is Rust's job.
  * `attention/default_prefill_params.cuh` loses its six `__host__`
    constructors, and `page.cuh` and `fastdiv.cuh` theirs, for the reason
    above: NVRTC rejects the *declaration*, not just the body. The structs and
    their `__host__ __device__` accessors -- which is what a kernel reads out
    of `__grid_constant__` -- are untouched, and the fields are filled from
    Rust.

### What is NOT FlashInfer's, and no longer sits next to it

The six small headers PIE wrote to stand in for names NVRTC does not ship --

    cstdint, type_traits, cuda.h, cuda_runtime.h, bit,
    boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp

-- used to share a directory with the FlashInfer text, which made this a
paragraph about disambiguation. They are now in `csrc/shim/`, a directory that
holds nothing else and whose README states the rule, so the disambiguation is
structural and this paragraph is only history. There are twenty-one of them
today; `csrc/shim/README.md` is the register, and it records each addition and
retirement rather than restating a total.

They are named after the headers they stand in for because an `#include`
resolves against a header set by exact spelling -- this crate has no include
path on disk, so the spelling in the directive *is* the identity of the
header. Each one exists because guarding its include was tried and measured to
break device code, and each says so in its own banner. In particular
`boost/math/ccmath/fabs.hpp` contains no Boost code and is not derived from
Boost: it is a one-line constexpr absolute value under a name FlashInfer asks
for.

### Where it lives, and why it moved

`csrc/src/attn/flashinfer/`, subtree shape exactly as upstream ships it, plus
`LICENSE` and `MODIFICATIONS` at its root. It was `csrc/vendor/flashinfer/`.

The move is the point, not a side effect: this crate compiles every kernel
through NVRTC, so `csrc/` is device text and nothing else, and a directory
called `vendor` said the opposite -- that the text was a guest with its own
rules. It is not a guest. It is compiled by the same JIT, guarded by the same
markers, and tested by the same tests as everything beside it.

**Not one byte of the twenty-nine changed in the move** -- all 28 source files
and `LICENSE`, zero changed lines each; only `MODIFICATIONS`, which is PIE's
own file, was edited, to record the new path. The subtree shape is what makes
that true: upstream's files include each other relatively (`"../cp_async.cuh"`,
`"mask.cuh"`) and a relative spelling survives a move that carries the whole
tree.

Seventeen `#include` directives changed across the whole internalisation, and
the split is worth stating because only one half of it touches upstream text:
**twelve in PIE's own files** -- ten `<flashinfer/attention/…>` and two
`<xqa/…>`, which is where an angled, tree-rooted spelling lived -- and **five
inside the XQA files themselves**, which had already been made tree-relative
for an unrelated reason (see the XQA section below) and simply gained the new
prefix. No FlashInfer file contains a changed directive.


